!! Discovering port

There're a number of ports an =EDAApplication= needs: event publisher, event store, projections databases, and so on. Also, different =EDAApplication=s might need some of them, but not all, but we'll leave that for later.

What we are pursuing first is to write a new port and adapter, and make it automatically appear in the SettingsBrowser.

Recall that the logic that makes that happen is in =EDASettings class >> portSettingsPort:under:on:order:=:

[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASettings class >> portSettingsOn: aBuilder under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	| entry environmentValues key portResolver commandListenerValues |
	key := aParent , 'ports'.
	entry := aBuilder group: key.
	entry
		label: 'Ports' translated;
		parent: aParent.
	portResolver := EDASPortResolver new.
	environmentValues := OrderedCollection withAll:
		                     ((portResolver
			                       availableEnvironmentSettingsAdapters
			                       collect: [ :adapterClass |
			                       adapterClass description translated
			                       -> adapterClass ]) sorted: [ :a :b | a < b ]).
	(aBuilder pickOne: #environmentSettingsAdapterClass)
		label: 'Environment adapter' translated;
		target: app portSettings;
		parent: key;
		domainValues: environmentValues;
		order: 1.
	commandListenerValues := OrderedCollection withAll:
		                         ((portResolver
			                           availableCommandListenerSettingsAdapters
			                           collect: [ :adapterClass |
			                           adapterClass description translated
			                           -> adapterClass ]) sorted: [ :a :b |
			                          a < b ]).
	(aBuilder pickOne: #commandListenerSettingsAdapterClass)
		label: 'Command Listener adapter' translated;
		target: app portSettings;
		parent: key;
		domainValues: commandListenerValues;
		order: 2
]]]

We could introduce an indirection in =EDASPortResolver= so it accepts a symbol:

% ${changes:path=code/001-EDASSettings_portSettingsOn_under_on_order_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASettings class >> portSettingsOn: aBuilder under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	| entry key portResolver |
	key := aParent , 'ports'.
	entry := aBuilder group: key.
	entry
		label: 'Ports' translated;
		parent: aParent.
	portResolver := EDASPortResolver new.
	#( 'environment' 'commandListener' ) asOrderedCollection
		keysAndValuesDo: [ :i :p |
			| values |
			values := OrderedCollection withAll:
				          (((portResolver availableAdaptersFor: p) collect: [
					            :adapterClass |
					            adapterClass description translated -> adapterClass ])
					           sorted: [ :a :b | a < b ]).
			values ifNotEmpty: [
				(aBuilder pickOne: (portResolver portAdapterClassSymbol: p))
					label: (portResolver portDescription: p) translated;
					target: app portSettings;
					parent: key;
					domainValues: values;
					order: i ] ]
]]]

We've just introduced three new methods we need to implement, all in =EDASPortResolver=: =availableAdapters:=, =portToAdapterClassSymbol:= and =portDescription:=.
To improve flexibility and decouple the ports and adapters from the base PharoEDA framework, we'll use Pragmas. Recall that Pragmas are method-level annotations. Let's start by annotating the existing ports. Since we are requesting each port its values, we can annotate that method directly.

% ${changes:path=code/002-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_values_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort >> values: aSymbol

	<edaPort: #environment>
	^ self availableEnvironments
]]]

Before continuing, let's create a test.

% ${changes:path=code/003-EDASPortResolverTest.ombu}
[[[classDefinition=true
TestCase subclass: #EDASPortResolverTest
	instanceVariableNames: ''
	classVariableNames: ''
	package: 'EDAS-Settings-Ports-Tests'
]]]

We now need to create the new package, and add it to the baseline. Don't forget to add it to the repository as well.

% ${changes:path=code/004-BaselineOfPharoEDASettings_declarePackagesIn_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
BaselineOfPharoEDASettings >> declarePackagesIn: spec

	spec
		package: #'EDAS-Settings'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'DeployUtils' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Tests'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'EDAS-Settings' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Ports'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-InMemory'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-EnvVars'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-Json'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-Json-Tests'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Ports-Tests'
		with: [ spec requires: #( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' ) ];
		package: #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-ByPriority' with: [
			spec requires:
					#( 'PharoEDACommon' 'EDAS-Settings-Ports' 'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-InMemory'
					   'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-EnvVars'
					   'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-Json' ) ]
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/005-BaselineOfPharoEDASettings_declareGroupsIn_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
BaselineOfPharoEDASettings >> declareGroupsIn: spec

	spec
		group: 'core'
		with:
			#( #'EDAS-Settings' #'EDAS-Settings-Ports' #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-InMemory'
			   #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-EnvVars'
			   #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-Json' #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-ByPriority' );
		group: 'tests'
		with:
			#( #'EDAS-Settings-Tests' #'EDAS-Settings-Adapters-Json-Tests'
			   #'EDAS-Settings-Ports-Tests' );
		group: 'default' with: #( 'core' 'tests' )
]]]

The first method to implement is =portAdapterClassSymbol:= in the port resolver trait. Let's start with a test.

% ${changes:path=code/006-EDASPortResolverTest_testPortAdapterClassSymbol.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASPortResolverTest >> testPortAdapterClassSymbol

	| sut |
	sut := EDASPortResolver new.
	self
		assert: (sut portAdapterClassSymbol: #environment)
		equals: #environmentSettingsAdapterClass
]]]

The implementation just defines the convention. No magic here.

% ${changes:path=code/007-EDASTPortResolver_portAdapterClassSymbol_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASTPortResolver >> portAdapterClassSymbol: aString

	^ (aString , 'SettingsAdapterClass') asSymbol
]]]

The next method is responsible of providing a description.

% ${changes:path=code/008-EDASPortResolverTest_testPortDescription.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASPortResolverTest >> testPortDescription

	| sut |
	sut := EDASPortResolver new.
	self
		assert: (sut portDescription: #environment)
		equals: 'Environment'
]]]

In this case we are not going to impose any convention. Each port is responsible for providing its own description, using a pragma.

% ${changes:path=code/009-EDASTPortResolver_availableAdaptersFor_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASTPortResolver >> availableAdaptersFor: aSymbol

	^ ((Pragma allNamed: #edaPort:as:)
		   select: [ :p | p arguments first = aSymbol ]
		   thenCollect: [ :p | p methodClass ]) asOrderedCollection
]]]

To make the tests pass, we need to add the annotation.

% ${changes:path=code/010-EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort_values_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort >> values: aSymbol

	<edaPort: #environment as: 'Environment'>
	^ self availableEnvironments
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/011-EDASTPortResolver_portDescription_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASTPortResolver >> portDescription: aSymbol

	^ ((Pragma allNamed: #edaPort:as:)
		   select: [ :p | p arguments first = aSymbol ]
		   thenCollect: [ :p | p arguments second ]) asOrderedCollection
		  ifEmpty: [ nil ]
		  ifNotEmpty: [ :c | c first ]
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/012-EDASPortResolverTest_testAvailableAdaptersFor.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASPortResolverTest >> testAvailableAdaptersFor

	| sut |
	sut := EDASPortResolver new.
	self
		assert: (sut availableAdaptersFor: #environment)
		equals: (OrderedCollection withAll:
				 (sut availableAdaptersForPort: EDASEnvironmentSettingsPort))
]]]

The implementation is similar as the =portDescription:= we did previously.

% ${changes:path=code/013-EDASTPortResolver_availableAdaptersFor_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASTPortResolver >> availableAdaptersFor: aSymbol

	^ ((Pragma allNamed: #edaPort:as:)
		   select: [ :p | p arguments first = aSymbol ]
		   thenCollect: [ :p | p methodClass ]) asOrderedCollection
		  ifEmpty: [ OrderedCollection new ]
		  ifNotEmpty: [ :c | self availableAdaptersForPort: c first ]
]]]

If we launch now a SettingBrowser we can check the environment port is detected.
The command listener is not yet, since we haven't added the pragma to the port. In this case, we'll add it to another method.

% ${changes:path=code/014-EDASCommandListenerSettingsPort_availableErrorStrategies.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASCommandListenerSettingsPort >> availableErrorStrategies

	<edaPort: #commandListener as: 'Command listener'>
	self subclassResponsibility
]]]
